dict(t="2026-10-15 12:31", fr=J, to=M, subj="Re: How was it?", kind="full", body="""It was fun when giving the test. Some of the good students came up with a temperature ruse of 205 F and thought that was a really low number.... and so checked their work a few times. Then they came up to me (during the test) and wondered if they did something wrong because it was such a low number. I smiled and said, "maybe that's what it was."

It's the beauty of simplifying the problem with approximations to get a ballpark idea of what was going on that I was trying to teach them.

Notice in the last two pages are from the April 15 meeting, and were presented by G. Scott Hubbard. Watching the progress of the CAIB panel, I lived through that guy. He's also an experimentalist. I'm guessing he also saw it the way I did. The head of the panel (Gehman) wanted everyone to go through their fault tree, looking at everything. At the very end, when Scott Hubbard set up the final test, first on a fiberglass mock up, the the actual wing of another shuttle that had flown a similar number of f;ogjts. Hubbard set it up perfectly. With the fiberglass wing, he saw he had the aim correct. (You only have one go at the real wing.) Then when he ran the final test and on the real wing, he produced the exact same damage as was on the Shuttle..... as determined by the piece that fell out when the Shuttle did it's roll. Hubbard threw up both arms like an official does to note a "touchdown" in football. When he called Gehman on the phone to tell him the results, Gehman said to Hubbard, "I'll bet you're grinning from ear to ear right now."
For me, that sounded like Hubbard had predicted this all along -- and Gehman knew that.
I'd feel the same way. I think that was an amazing test Hubbard set up."""),
