dict(t="2026-10-17 11:31", fr=M, to=J, subj="Re: I FOUND IT!!!! The view of the foam on STS 107", kind="full",
     img=[("columbia-on-the-pad.jpg", "Columbia on the launch pad, from the Wikipedia article: the red circles mark the part of the external tank the foam came from and where it hit the left wing")],
     body="""Oh right, haha somehow I connected your carbon-carbon comment to that wiki link I was reviewing hahaha I usually get all the info and then put them in my OneNote document that I will then review later for making / filming the tutorial. But looking at the videos and comparing with your exam 5 days later, it's astonishing how accurate you were! (part of me thinks NASA was sidelining a bit to syphon tax-payer money haha).

Here is the Space Shuttle Columbia disaster wiki: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_Shuttle_Columbia_disaster They include a lot of the clips from those videos, including highlighting the part that flew off and hit the wing:"""),
dict(t="2026-10-17 12:20", fr=J, to=M, subj="Re: I FOUND IT!!!! The view of the foam on STS 107", kind="full", body="""The fiberglass test was a "dress rehearsal" before the Atlantis wing. No do-overs with the real wing.
I vaguely remember they had 3 fiberglass wings to practice on. In my lab, I call it "doing an experiment on the experiment." You go through all the set-up to see what might go wrong.... and how it all works. The last fiberglass test looked just like the actual wing test. I think most of the wing was fiberglass, but the test area was carbon carbon -- which id why it behaved the same.

I knew that day what the likely problem was. The scheduled test was 5 days later, so I had time to make the drawings. ;-)
I was angry about what happened and wanted to be able to say that my EM304 students could even figure it out. ;-)

A few months after the test, I brought a copy of it to a conference, hoping to see a guy I knew from NASA -- who was involved in some of the behind-the-scenes stuff. I noticed his name on some of the email copies that were posted. When I proudly showed him the test paper, he replied, "At least there was some good that came from this." ;-) (More to that story, but it will need to wait.)

One of the shuttle crew was accomplished in gymnastics. I figured he'd know how to move his body to look at the wing. They had space suits, but no cord. They could have duct-taped a cord to his boots and let him go look. ;-)

But the sickest part was that they did not notify the crew of the seriousness. There was going to be a press conference with the crew, so NASA told them about an "insignificant" foam strike, in case the media asked them about it.
I remember that there was a "care package" (my term) to be launched soon after the Shuttle took off. It was intended for the space station, but they could easily have re-aimed it to where the shuttle was. That would have kept the crew supplied with food, water, oxygen, etc. until it was determined what to do.... at a minimum.

It was also tragic that three different groups had requested spy-satellite imaging of the shuttle wing. Each rrequest was cancelled -- as soon as the higher-ups found out about it. One group almost pulled it off. But the imaging folks contacted NASA to ask where to send the images. "Images? What images??" And it got cancelled.

NASA management didn't want proof that they knew of the damage. But it was obvious they knew. Imagine how expensive all that would have been. So some idiots decided to cross their fingers and hope for the best.

What I did for my Master's degree thesis was actually for the cargo-bay doors. But I was familiar with the whole outer shell of the shuttle. The composite lay-up for the CBDs was such that the coefficient of thermal expansion would be zero -- if the composite was whole. What I was testing was the change in thermal expansion properties if there was a defect in the laminate. I would jokingly describe it as if the guys setting up the laminate wanted to go to the pop machine to get some pop, so reached in their pockets st see if they had enough change.... and lint came out and fell into one of the layers of the laminate. (In reality, they of course work in a clean room. But enexpected things may occur and it may go unnoticed.

So of course I've had a healthy respect for thermal expansion. Most people don't think of it. Example: pop cans exploding in the freezer. Frozen garden hoses with water in them. Garden hoses under pressure on a hot summer day. (Ryan blew up my hose by leaving it on under pressure in early August. He thought I was just being obsessive when I insisted he turn off the water and relieve pressure after using it.)

DJ"""),
